{#
  Error fragment (#843, epic #788) — swapped into ``#results`` when a run fails
  on user input. NOT a full page; it extends nothing.

  Renders a sanitised, human-readable account of what went wrong and never a
  stack trace. Two, non-exclusive, sources are supported:

    * ``field_errors`` — a list of ``{field, message}`` items derived by the
      route from a pydantic ``ValidationError`` (the route flattens each entry's
      ``loc``/``msg``; the raw exception, input values, and internal types are
      NOT passed through);
    * ``message`` — a single summary line for a domain error the engine raises
      by contract (``ConfigValidationError`` / ``AepProvenanceError`` /
      ``AepReconciliationError``).

  The route returns this fragment with HTTP 200 or 422 for bad user input — never
  a 500 — so htmx swaps it in place of the results.

  Security: rendered under an ``autoescape = True`` environment; field names and
  messages are HTML-escaped.
#}
<article class="results-panel results-panel--error" role="alert">
  <header class="results-panel__header">
    <h2 class="results-panel__title results-panel__title--error">
      Could not run the case
    </h2>
  </header>

  <div class="banner banner--error">
    Please correct the inputs below and run again.
  </div>

  {% if message %}
  <p class="error-summary">{{ message }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if field_errors %}
  <ul class="error-list">
    {% for item in field_errors %}
    <li class="error-list__item">
      <span class="error-list__field">{{ item.field }}</span>
      <span class="error-list__message">{{ item.message }}</span>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  {% elif not message %}
  <p class="error-summary">The submitted inputs were not valid.</p>
  {% endif %}
</article>
